1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,186
Dave has found his dream house.

2
00:00:02,186 --> 00:00:03,385
Small. Blue door.

3
00:00:03,385 --> 00:00:05,643
A tree that only drops leaves on

4
00:00:05,643 --> 00:00:07,124
the neighbour's side.

5
00:00:07,404 --> 00:00:08,877
Dave goes to the bank.

6
00:00:08,877 --> 00:00:11,689
Linda, the loan officer, types his numbers

7
00:00:11,689 --> 00:00:12,894
into the computer.

8
00:00:13,174 --> 00:00:15,222
A year ago, she says, your mortgage

9
00:00:15,222 --> 00:00:17,329
rate would have been about six point

10
00:00:17,329 --> 00:00:18,031
two percent.

11
00:00:18,311 --> 00:00:20,635
Today it's seven point three two.

12
00:00:20,635 --> 00:00:22,465
Dave asks if that's a lot.

13
00:00:22,465 --> 00:00:24,578
Linda turns the screen around.

14
00:00:24,858 --> 00:00:26,997
Dave reads the monthly payment.

15
00:00:26,997 --> 00:00:29,344
Dave sits down on the floor, which

16
00:00:29,344 --> 00:00:31,207
is not a chair, but is very

17
00:00:31,207 --> 00:00:31,690
stable.

18
00:00:31,970 --> 00:00:34,329
Then Linda adds: and on Wednesday, the

19
00:00:34,329 --> 00:00:37,060
Federal Reserve might decide whether to make

20
00:00:37,060 --> 00:00:38,922
borrowing even more expensive.

21
00:00:39,202 --> 00:00:41,934
Today: why is America's central bank thinking

22
00:00:41,934 --> 00:00:44,908
about raising interest rates while people already

23
00:00:44,908 --> 00:00:46,912
feel broke? And what does it mean

24
00:00:46,912 --> 00:00:47,883
for your wallet?

25
00:00:48,163 --> 00:00:49,169
First, the Fed.

26
00:00:49,169 --> 00:00:52,252
The Federal Reserve is America's central bank.

27
00:00:52,252 --> 00:00:54,062
It doesn't give you a loan.

28
00:00:54,062 --> 00:00:56,341
It sets the price of borrowing for

29
00:00:56,341 --> 00:00:57,279
everyone else.

30
00:00:57,559 --> 00:00:59,814
Think of it as a thermostat for

31
00:00:59,814 --> 00:01:02,506
the economy. When things run too hot,

32
00:01:02,506 --> 00:01:03,815
prices climb fast.

33
00:01:03,815 --> 00:01:06,507
When things run too cold, people lose

34
00:01:06,507 --> 00:01:06,870
jobs.

35
00:01:07,150 --> 00:01:09,720
Its main dial is a short-term interest

36
00:01:09,720 --> 00:01:11,682
rate, the federal funds rate.

37
00:01:11,682 --> 00:01:14,184
Right now it sits between three point

38
00:01:14,184 --> 00:01:16,078
seven five and four percent.

39
00:01:16,358 --> 00:01:18,622
Turn the dial up, and loans for

40
00:01:18,622 --> 00:01:21,543
houses, cars and businesses get pricier.

41
00:01:21,543 --> 00:01:24,683
People borrow less, spend less, and prices,

42
00:01:24,683 --> 00:01:26,217
in theory, calm down.

43
00:01:26,497 --> 00:01:29,248
Turn it down, and borrowing gets cheaper.

44
00:01:29,248 --> 00:01:31,798
More spending, more hiring, but also a

45
00:01:31,798 --> 00:01:34,012
risk of prices running hot again.

46
00:01:34,292 --> 00:01:37,114
The Fed's target is simple: inflation of

47
00:01:37,114 --> 00:01:38,454
two percent a year.

48
00:01:38,454 --> 00:01:39,441
Not zero. Two.

49
00:01:39,721 --> 00:01:41,413
The Fed is also run by a

50
00:01:41,413 --> 00:01:43,246
committee, not one person.

51
00:01:43,246 --> 00:01:44,938
Twelve voting officials.

52
00:01:44,938 --> 00:01:47,194
They meet eight times a year and

53
00:01:47,194 --> 00:01:48,886
argue politely in suits.

54
00:01:49,166 --> 00:01:51,637
The chair, since May, is Kevin Warsh.

55
00:01:51,637 --> 00:01:53,974
Here he's a generic stick figure in

56
00:01:53,974 --> 00:01:56,178
a tie, because we don't draw real

57
00:01:56,178 --> 00:01:56,645
people.

58
00:01:56,925 --> 00:01:58,822
So why is the dial even being

59
00:01:58,822 --> 00:02:01,045
discussed? Because of this number.

60
00:02:01,325 --> 00:02:04,314
In September, consumer prices were three point

61
00:02:04,314 --> 00:02:06,978
seven percent higher than a year earlier.

62
00:02:06,978 --> 00:02:09,187
That's up from three point four in

63
00:02:09,187 --> 00:02:09,642
August.

64
00:02:09,922 --> 00:02:12,280
In just one month, prices rose point

65
00:02:12,280 --> 00:02:14,639
six percent. Do that every month for

66
00:02:14,639 --> 00:02:16,604
a year and you're above seven.

67
00:02:16,884 --> 00:02:18,157
The target is two.

68
00:02:18,157 --> 00:02:20,986
Inflation has now been above two percent

69
00:02:20,986 --> 00:02:23,603
for five years, according to a Forbes

70
00:02:23,603 --> 00:02:24,240
analysis.

71
00:02:24,520 --> 00:02:26,819
The biggest driver is energy.

72
00:02:26,819 --> 00:02:29,356
Since the Iran war began in late

73
00:02:29,356 --> 00:02:32,369
February, the average gallon of gas is

74
00:02:32,369 --> 00:02:36,016
up about forty-three percent, according to AAA

75
00:02:36,016 --> 00:02:37,046
via ABC News.

76
00:02:37,326 --> 00:02:40,696
Today's average is four dollars twenty-nine.

77
00:02:40,696 --> 00:02:43,760
And on Friday, ABC reported, oil crossed

78
00:02:43,760 --> 00:02:46,747
one hundred sixteen dollars a barrel, a

79
00:02:46,747 --> 00:02:47,896
six-month high.

80
00:02:48,176 --> 00:02:50,434
Dave's car now has a fuel gauge

81
00:02:50,434 --> 00:02:53,565
with only two settings: empty, and recently

82
00:02:53,565 --> 00:02:54,002
empty.

83
00:02:54,282 --> 00:02:55,373
Here's the twist.

84
00:02:55,373 --> 00:02:57,426
A year ago, the Fed was cutting.

85
00:02:57,426 --> 00:02:59,736
At the end of twenty twenty-five, it

86
00:02:59,736 --> 00:03:02,046
lowered rates by three quarters of a

87
00:03:02,046 --> 00:03:03,137
percentage point.

88
00:03:03,417 --> 00:03:05,005
Then prices heated up.

89
00:03:05,005 --> 00:03:07,819
In July, three Fed officials dissented.

90
00:03:07,819 --> 00:03:09,190
They wanted a hike.

91
00:03:09,190 --> 00:03:11,138
The majority said: not yet.

92
00:03:11,418 --> 00:03:14,436
On September sixteenth, the Fed raised rates

93
00:03:14,436 --> 00:03:15,739
by a quarter point.

94
00:03:15,739 --> 00:03:18,003
Its first hike in more than three

95
00:03:18,003 --> 00:03:18,415
years.

96
00:03:18,695 --> 00:03:21,448
At that meeting, most officials pencilled in

97
00:03:21,448 --> 00:03:22,949
one more hike this year.

98
00:03:22,949 --> 00:03:24,638
A handful pencilled in two.

99
00:03:24,918 --> 00:03:27,457
Forbes points out that those pencil marks

100
00:03:27,457 --> 00:03:29,687
are already out of date, because the

101
00:03:29,687 --> 00:03:32,411
inflation numbers since then came in hotter.

102
00:03:32,691 --> 00:03:35,233
It's like planning a picnic on Tuesday,

103
00:03:35,233 --> 00:03:38,362
then seeing Wednesday's forecast is a hailstorm.

104
00:03:38,362 --> 00:03:40,448
The plan is still on the fridge.

105
00:03:40,448 --> 00:03:41,490
It's just wrong.

106
00:03:41,770 --> 00:03:44,239
So, Wednesday, October twenty-eighth.

107
00:03:44,239 --> 00:03:46,307
Will the Fed raise rates again?

108
00:03:46,587 --> 00:03:49,026
Probably not, according to markets.

109
00:03:49,026 --> 00:03:52,091
On Friday, futures traders priced only about

110
00:03:52,091 --> 00:03:54,669
an eight percent chance of an October

111
00:03:54,669 --> 00:03:57,037
hike, according to Charles Schwab.

112
00:03:57,317 --> 00:03:59,217
But ask about the whole rest of

113
00:03:59,217 --> 00:04:00,872
the year, and the odds of a

114
00:04:00,872 --> 00:04:03,263
hike jump to about eighty-four percent.

115
00:04:03,543 --> 00:04:05,829
The date most people are circling is

116
00:04:05,829 --> 00:04:08,688
December ninth. That's the next meeting, when

117
00:04:08,688 --> 00:04:11,102
officials also update their forecasts.

118
00:04:11,382 --> 00:04:15,426
J.P. Morgan Wealth Management strategists expect about

119
00:04:15,426 --> 00:04:18,047
half a percentage point of hikes in

120
00:04:18,047 --> 00:04:20,069
total before the Fed stops.

121
00:04:20,069 --> 00:04:21,267
Timing: unclear.

122
00:04:21,547 --> 00:04:24,132
And bond markets are already pricing more

123
00:04:24,132 --> 00:04:27,473
hikes into early twenty twenty-seven, with short-term

124
00:04:27,473 --> 00:04:30,247
rates possibly reaching around five percent.

125
00:04:30,527 --> 00:04:33,274
New York Fed President John Williams has

126
00:04:33,274 --> 00:04:35,953
said officials may have, quote, no need

127
00:04:35,953 --> 00:04:38,426
for urgency. Which is Fed for: we're

128
00:04:38,426 --> 00:04:40,487
watching, we're not panicking.

129
00:04:40,767 --> 00:04:43,083
One thing makes this harder to guess.

130
00:04:43,083 --> 00:04:45,524
Chair Warsh has moved away from forward

131
00:04:45,524 --> 00:04:48,154
guidance, meaning the Fed hints less about

132
00:04:48,154 --> 00:04:49,343
what's coming next.

133
00:04:49,623 --> 00:04:51,775
Markets used to get a trailer before

134
00:04:51,775 --> 00:04:53,689
the movie. Now they get a poster

135
00:04:53,689 --> 00:04:55,303
with the title blacked out.

136
00:04:55,583 --> 00:04:57,721
Now, the argument inside the Fed.

137
00:04:57,721 --> 00:04:59,989
Depending on who you ask, there are

138
00:04:59,989 --> 00:05:01,738
two very different worries.

139
00:05:02,018 --> 00:05:03,842
Worry number one: prices.

140
00:05:03,842 --> 00:05:06,835
Inflation at three point seven percent is

141
00:05:06,835 --> 00:05:09,462
well over target, and it's been over

142
00:05:09,462 --> 00:05:10,702
target for years.

143
00:05:10,982 --> 00:05:13,861
If people start expecting high prices to

144
00:05:13,861 --> 00:05:16,668
last, they ask for bigger raises, shops

145
00:05:16,668 --> 00:05:19,259
raise prices to match, and the fever

146
00:05:19,259 --> 00:05:20,195
feeds itself.

147
00:05:20,475 --> 00:05:23,084
And people are starting to expect that.

148
00:05:23,084 --> 00:05:25,961
Americans now expect about three point five

149
00:05:25,961 --> 00:05:28,837
percent inflation over the next five years,

150
00:05:28,837 --> 00:05:31,045
up from three point two in March.

151
00:05:31,325 --> 00:05:34,799
That's from the University of Michigan survey.

152
00:05:34,799 --> 00:05:38,122
Its director, Joanne Hsu, wrote that, quote,

153
00:05:38,122 --> 00:05:41,822
consumers broadly expect inflation to accelerate.

154
00:05:42,102 --> 00:05:43,840
Worry number two: jobs.

155
00:05:43,840 --> 00:05:47,996
In September, employers added only twenty-nine thousand

156
00:05:47,996 --> 00:05:50,566
jobs. In August it was one hundred

157
00:05:50,566 --> 00:05:52,001
sixty-two thousand.

158
00:05:52,281 --> 00:05:54,900
Raise rates into a cooling job market,

159
00:05:54,900 --> 00:05:57,381
this side says, and you risk turning

160
00:05:57,381 --> 00:05:59,036
a slowdown into layoffs.

161
00:05:59,316 --> 00:06:02,095
They also note this inflation is mostly

162
00:06:02,095 --> 00:06:05,303
energy. Core inflation, which leaves out food

163
00:06:05,303 --> 00:06:07,869
and fuel, was two point six percent.

164
00:06:07,869 --> 00:06:09,437
Much closer to target.

165
00:06:09,717 --> 00:06:12,181
And here's the awkward part: a higher

166
00:06:12,181 --> 00:06:14,844
interest rate can't open a shipping lane

167
00:06:14,844 --> 00:06:17,108
or make an oil tanker sail faster.

168
00:06:17,388 --> 00:06:20,157
The other side answers: true, but energy

169
00:06:20,157 --> 00:06:22,025
costs leak into everything.

170
00:06:22,025 --> 00:06:25,140
Forbes notes higher energy prices may already

171
00:06:25,140 --> 00:06:27,977
be spreading to other goods and services.

172
00:06:28,257 --> 00:06:29,792
Both sides have a point.

173
00:06:29,792 --> 00:06:31,519
That's why the room argues.

174
00:06:31,519 --> 00:06:33,054
We just count the facts.

175
00:06:33,334 --> 00:06:35,216
So what does this mean for you?

176
00:06:35,216 --> 00:06:36,309
Start with houses.

177
00:06:36,589 --> 00:06:39,815
Freddie Mac says the average thirty-year mortgage

178
00:06:39,815 --> 00:06:42,251
fell to seven point three two percent

179
00:06:42,251 --> 00:06:44,753
this week, from seven point four zero.

180
00:06:44,753 --> 00:06:46,530
Its first dip since August.

181
00:06:46,810 --> 00:06:50,142
Freddie Mac's chief economist called it, quote,

182
00:06:50,142 --> 00:06:52,836
some relief to prospective homebuyers.

183
00:06:52,836 --> 00:06:55,034
Some. Like a single ice cube in

184
00:06:55,034 --> 00:06:55,388
July.

185
00:06:55,668 --> 00:06:57,610
A year ago, that rate was six

186
00:06:57,610 --> 00:06:58,614
point one nine.

187
00:06:58,614 --> 00:07:00,958
On a big loan, that difference adds

188
00:07:00,958 --> 00:07:02,833
hundreds of dollars a month.

189
00:07:03,113 --> 00:07:06,564
Mortgages follow long-term bond yields more than

190
00:07:06,564 --> 00:07:08,289
the Fed's dial directly.

191
00:07:08,289 --> 00:07:11,237
The ten-year Treasury yield was near five

192
00:07:11,237 --> 00:07:13,897
percent on Friday, at four point nine

193
00:07:13,897 --> 00:07:14,185
six.

194
00:07:14,465 --> 00:07:16,206
Credit cards are different.

195
00:07:16,206 --> 00:07:18,850
Their rates move almost directly with the

196
00:07:18,850 --> 00:07:21,493
Fed. Another hike, and carrying a balance

197
00:07:21,493 --> 00:07:23,363
gets more expensive, quickly.

198
00:07:23,643 --> 00:07:26,218
Car loans and small business loans sit

199
00:07:26,218 --> 00:07:27,641
somewhere in between.

200
00:07:27,641 --> 00:07:29,810
They feel it, just with a delay.

201
00:07:30,090 --> 00:07:31,439
There is one winner.

202
00:07:31,439 --> 00:07:34,743
Savers. Higher rates usually mean better interest

203
00:07:34,743 --> 00:07:36,092
on savings accounts.

204
00:07:36,372 --> 00:07:38,316
Linda has a savings account.

205
00:07:38,316 --> 00:07:40,191
Linda is quietly delighted.

206
00:07:40,191 --> 00:07:42,691
Linda will not be telling Dave this.

207
00:07:42,971 --> 00:07:45,887
Meanwhile, Americans already feel squeezed.

208
00:07:45,887 --> 00:07:49,210
The University of Michigan's sentiment index came

209
00:07:49,210 --> 00:07:51,176
in at forty-eight in October.

210
00:07:51,456 --> 00:07:54,471
That's the second-lowest final reading in the

211
00:07:54,471 --> 00:07:56,816
survey's seventy-four year history.

212
00:07:56,816 --> 00:07:59,697
The record low, forty-four point eight, was

213
00:07:59,697 --> 00:08:00,568
set this May.

214
00:08:00,848 --> 00:08:03,560
Sentiment is now ten percent below a

215
00:08:03,560 --> 00:08:06,949
year ago, and fifteen percent below February,

216
00:08:06,949 --> 00:08:09,963
just before the Iran conflict, Hsu said.

217
00:08:10,243 --> 00:08:13,866
Her summary, quote: purchasing power continues to

218
00:08:13,866 --> 00:08:16,750
dominate consumer views of the economy.

219
00:08:16,750 --> 00:08:19,265
Translation: stuff costs too much.

220
00:08:19,545 --> 00:08:22,431
Prices worry people across the spectrum.

221
00:08:22,431 --> 00:08:25,750
Since January, the share of Republicans citing

222
00:08:25,750 --> 00:08:30,801
inflation pressures rose fifty-two percent, independents twenty-seven,

223
00:08:30,801 --> 00:08:32,100
Democrats fifteen.

224
00:08:32,380 --> 00:08:34,144
And there's an election.

225
00:08:34,144 --> 00:08:37,009
The midterms are on November third, and

226
00:08:37,009 --> 00:08:40,095
ABC reports affordability is a top concern

227
00:08:40,095 --> 00:08:40,903
for voters.

228
00:08:41,183 --> 00:08:43,370
The Fed is meant to be independent

229
00:08:43,370 --> 00:08:46,007
of elections. Whatever it does next week,

230
00:08:46,007 --> 00:08:48,193
expect both sides to have a strong

231
00:08:48,193 --> 00:08:49,287
opinion about it.

232
00:08:49,567 --> 00:08:50,480
What to watch.

233
00:08:50,480 --> 00:08:53,545
Wednesday: the decision itself, and whether any

234
00:08:53,545 --> 00:08:56,024
officials dissent in favour of a hike.

235
00:08:56,024 --> 00:08:57,850
That would hint at December.

236
00:08:58,130 --> 00:09:01,155
Thursday: the economy's growth number for the

237
00:09:01,155 --> 00:09:04,450
summer, plus the Fed's favourite inflation gauge,

238
00:09:04,450 --> 00:09:05,324
called P C E.

239
00:09:05,604 --> 00:09:08,200
Then oil. If tankers move freely again

240
00:09:08,200 --> 00:09:10,934
and energy falls, the pressure for hikes

241
00:09:10,934 --> 00:09:13,735
eases. If oil keeps climbing, it doesn't.

242
00:09:14,015 --> 00:09:16,580
And December ninth: the meeting the markets

243
00:09:16,580 --> 00:09:17,713
think matters most.

244
00:09:17,993 --> 00:09:20,806
Quick recap. Prices are rising at three

245
00:09:20,806 --> 00:09:24,123
point seven percent, mostly because of energy.

246
00:09:24,123 --> 00:09:27,008
The Fed already hiked once in September.

247
00:09:27,288 --> 00:09:30,020
Markets expect a pause on Wednesday and

248
00:09:30,020 --> 00:09:31,772
another hike by December.

249
00:09:31,772 --> 00:09:35,064
Officials are split between fighting prices and

250
00:09:35,064 --> 00:09:36,185
protecting jobs.

251
00:09:36,465 --> 00:09:38,841
For you: mortgages near seven and a

252
00:09:38,841 --> 00:09:42,167
third percent, pricier credit cards, and slightly

253
00:09:42,167 --> 00:09:43,186
happier savers.

254
00:09:43,466 --> 00:09:45,188
Back at the bank, Dave has a

255
00:09:45,188 --> 00:09:47,217
new plan. He'll wait for rates to

256
00:09:47,217 --> 00:09:49,124
fall, then buy the dream house.

257
00:09:49,404 --> 00:09:51,990
Linda points out the house has already

258
00:09:51,990 --> 00:09:53,487
sold. To a cash buyer.

259
00:09:53,487 --> 00:09:55,529
Who is, it turns out, a saver.

260
00:09:55,809 --> 00:09:57,644
Your turn. If you were on the

261
00:09:57,644 --> 00:10:00,490
Fed's committee on Wednesday, would you raise

262
00:10:00,490 --> 00:10:01,756
rates, hold, or cut?

263
00:10:01,756 --> 00:10:03,274
Tell us in the comments.

264
00:10:03,274 --> 00:10:04,602
Show your thermostat.

265
00:10:04,882 --> 00:10:07,797
If this helped, subscribe for calm, stick-figure

266
00:10:07,797 --> 00:10:09,619
news. See you at the next one.

